 #:seon.task{:name "define"
             :intent "## Problem\n\n`seon.fs/under-path?` answers false for a path under a root that ends in the separator: `(#'seon.fs/under-path? \"/\" \"/x\")` answers false where true is right.\n\n## What exists\n\n`seon.fs/under-path?` appends the separator to the ancestor before comparing prefixes; its caller `seon.fs/admit-destructive-path!` refuses a deletion outside its root with it. The table test `seon.fs-test/under-path-tells-what-lies-under-a-root` covers it.\n\n## Examples\n\n- `\"/\"` and `\"/x\"`: true; `\"/a/b/\"` and `\"/a/b/c\"`: true; `\"/a/b\"` and `\"/a/b\"`: true.\n- `\"/a/b\"` and `\"/a/bc\"`: false, a sibling sharing a prefix; `\"/a/b\"` under `\"/a/b/c\"`: false.\n\n## Expected cost\n\nOne string comparison per call, proportional to the path's length; no measured claim."
             :instructions "## Work\n\nGrow the subject's intent (keep what Survey wrote) with `my.task/refine!`: a `## Examples` section of exact inputs and the outputs they must give, boundary cases first (empty, equal, one past, the sibling that shares a prefix), and a `## Expected cost` section naming what the work is proportional to and the workload that measures it.\n\n## Example\n\nThis template's own intent is a worked example of the document as Define leaves it.\n\n## Done\n\nProblem and What exists still have content, Examples and Expected cost have content, and every reference resolves. A backticked deftest cited under Expected cost is a cost claim: it must exist and call `seon.profile/measure`, and its cost is compared with the stored costs at the merge base (`seon.db/stored-costs`, `seon.batch/cost-diff`), so a regression fails. An Expected cost section that cites no deftest makes no claim."}
 #:seon.task{:name "design"
             :intent "## Problem\n\n`seon.fs/under-path?` answers false for a path under a root that ends in the separator: `(#'seon.fs/under-path? \"/\" \"/x\")` answers false where true is right.\n\n## What exists\n\n`seon.fs/under-path?` appends the separator to the ancestor before comparing prefixes; its caller `seon.fs/admit-destructive-path!` refuses a deletion outside its root with it. The table test `seon.fs-test/under-path-tells-what-lies-under-a-root` covers it.\n\n## Examples\n\n- `\"/\"` and `\"/x\"`: true; `\"/a/b/\"` and `\"/a/b/c\"`: true; `\"/a/b\"` and `\"/a/b\"`: true.\n- `\"/a/b\"` and `\"/a/bc\"`: false, a sibling sharing a prefix; `\"/a/b\"` under `\"/a/b/c\"`: false.\n\n## Expected cost\n\nOne string comparison per call, proportional to the path's length; no measured claim.\n\n## Approach\n\nChange the producer `seon.fs/under-path?` only: an ancestor that already ends in the separator gets no second one. Its contract and its callers stay; nothing is added or deleted."
             :instructions "## Work\n\nGrow the subject's intent with `my.task/refine!`: an `## Approach` section naming the producer to change, each function's name, arity and contract over named schemas, what it replaces and deletes, and the callers that convert with it. Reuse the existing interfaces and library seams first. A function the approach needs that does not exist yet is a `defn` in its owner's namespace with its complete contract and the body `(throw (ex-info \"not yet\" {:seon.error/type :seon.error/unimplemented}))`, so later tests load against a real symbol; a test of such a stub asserts its instrumented input contract, never the temporary body.\n\n## Example\n\nThis template's own intent is a worked example of the document as Design leaves it.\n\n## Done\n\nThe sections before it keep their content, Approach has content, and every function cited under Approach resolves with a complete contract (`seon.contracts/gaps` answers none)."}
