             :instructions "## Work\n\nWrite the red deftests that show the subject done, one behavior each, asserting data: every exact and boundary case under Examples as a row of a table test (prefer a new row in an existing table test; a new deftest only for a behavior no test covers), and a cost assertion with `seon.profile/measure` for each cost claim under Expected cost. Tag each with the subject's task, `^{:seon/tasks #{'the.ns/subject}}`, and declare them as the subject's product with `my.task/refine!` (`:seon.task/product`, the set of their symbols): the product is what Implement makes green.\n\nWhere a general law holds (a round trip, an inverse, every path extended by a component is under it), write it as a property with a bounded generator and a fixed `:seed` beside a few table rows; a law catches a wrong row.\n\n## Example\n\n```clojure\n(deftest ^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.fs/a-path-under-a-root-answers-true}} under-path-tells-what-lies-under-a-root\n  (are [ancestor descendant answer] (= answer (#'seon.fs/under-path? ancestor descendant))\n    \"/\" \"/x\" true\n    \"/a/b\" \"/a/bc\" false))\n```\n\n## Done\n\nThe product is not empty, and every product test ran on your branch and failed by design: a failing assertion, or an error the code under test raised (`seon.test.runner/designed-red?`); a test that does not load ran nothing. Your own proofs are green, nothing you reach is broken, and every function on your branch is contracted, since the merge takes them all (unmap a scratch helper with `my.program/ns-unmap!`). The product is the whole set your Examples ask for, table rows and the law both: write all of them before you end; one red test does not finish the step."}
